Intuition
The key observation is that all rotten oranges spread the infection simultaneously every minute.

If we perform a separate DFS or BFS from each rotten orange, one source would finish spreading before another starts, which does not match the problem statement.

Instead, we treat every initially rotten orange as a starting point and begin a multi-source BFS. Since BFS explores nodes level by level, each level naturally represents one minute of infection spread.

The answer is simply the number of BFS levels required to rot all reachable fresh oranges.

Approach
Traverse the entire grid.
Push every initially rotten orange into the BFS queue with time 0.
Mark these oranges as visited.
Perform a BFS:
Remove the front orange.
Update the maximum time encountered.
Visit its four adjacent cells.
If a neighboring cell contains a fresh orange and hasn't been visited:
Mark it as visited.
Push it into the queue with time currentTime + 1.
After BFS finishes, traverse the grid once more.
If any fresh orange was never visited, it can never rot, so return -1.
Otherwise, return the maximum time recorded.
